DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppsseelleecctt() function was introduced by IEEE Std 1003.1g-2000
     (“POSIX.1”) as a slightly stronger version of select(2).  The _n_f_d_s,
     _r_e_a_d_f_d_s, _w_r_i_t_e_f_d_s, and _e_x_c_e_p_t_f_d_s arguments are all identical to the anal‐
     ogous arguments of sseelleecctt().  The _t_i_m_e_o_u_t argument in ppsseelleecctt() points to
     a _c_o_n_s_t _s_t_r_u_c_t _t_i_m_e_s_p_e_c rather than the potentially modifiable _s_t_r_u_c_t
     _t_i_m_e_v_a_l used by sseelleecctt(); as in sseelleecctt(), a null pointer may be passed to
     indicate that ppsseelleecctt() should wait indefinitely.  Finally, _n_e_w_s_i_g_m_a_s_k
     specifies a signal mask which is set while waiting for input.  When
     ppsseelleecctt() returns, the original signal mask is restored.  If _n_e_w_s_i_g_m_a_s_k
     is a null pointer, ppsseelleecctt() behaves like sseelleecctt() with no setting or
     restoration of the signal mask.

     See select(2) for a more detailed discussion of the semantics of this in‐
     terface, and for macros used to manipulate the _f_d___s_e_t data type.

